\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

% - FB's manipulation failures are associated with systematic errors in predicted future-state occupancy that obscure the relative value of actions needed for task success.
% - Control bottlenecks amplify FB's successor-estimation errors: these errors are larger around interactions where a restricted set of actions determines subsequent task progress.
% - The normalized parameterization and categorical Bellman objective used by \name{} reduce task-relevant successor-estimation errors and improve action rankings, particularly around the interactions identified in Section~\ref{sec:exp-bottlenecks}.
% - The improvements in successor estimation translate into higher task performance, with larger gains on tasks requiring the interactions for which prediction accuracy improves.
% - The learned successor representation supports useful policy selection for reward functions withheld during pretraining, including objectives that require different preferences over future states.

Our experiments test the argument above in four steps. %%AZ.9.12: be more specific, what argument are you testing? Do you just mean the 3 components you modify? just say that.
%%AZ.9.12: looks like we're actually missing this ablation in the experiments. I think it's important to have something that tests each of the 3 modifications to FB (I guess you might only be able to show this as two modifications, since the 3rd is meant to address the first)
First, we isolate the proposed failure in the controlled bottleneck MDP, where the exact successor measure and action gap are known (\cref{sec:exp-didactic}). %%AZ.9.12: what proposed failure
We then evaluate \name{} on multiple manipulation tasks across benchmarks on OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill in \cref{sec:exp-manipulation}.
\Cref{sec:reward-transfer} tests whether the same frozen representation remains useful when the downstream reward is specified only after pretraining.
\Cref{sec:exp-failure} examines the OGBench policies more closely to determine where FB fails and whether its successor predictions and action rankings explain those failures.

\paragraph{Experimental protocol.}
All methods use states, are trained for $10^6$ gradient steps on the same fixed offline dataset.
We report results over $5$ independent training seeds.
For offline policy learning, we use the actor from \citet{bagatella-iclr26a}, based on Flow Q-Learning~\citep{park-icml25a}.
Success rates are averaged over the final three evaluations, with $10$ episodes per task.
Full implementation details are given in \cref{app:implementation}.

