\subsection{Preserving the bottleneck decision}
\label{sec:exp-didactic}
%%AZ.9.12: this is the env where you should also conduct the ablations on Density-FB.
\aditya{Add more envs?}
The failure analysis makes a concrete prediction: a low-rank squared-loss successor model can fit most of the successor measure while losing the smaller difference that determines which action is preferred.
If \name{} addresses this failure, it should preserve this action difference at lower rank than standard FB.
We test this on the nine-state process from \cref{sec:failure}, where the exact successor measure and action values are known.
Both critics use the same fixed uniform continuation policy, transitions, and exact expectations, with no learned actor.

\paragraph{Making the bottleneck rare.}
Lemma~\ref{lem:rare-bottleneck} predicts that FB's reconstructed action gap at state $7$ collapses as the two state-$7$ rows lose weight in the objective, while the true gap does not move.
\Cref{fig:rare-bottleneck} tests this at rank $3$ in the two ways described in \cref{sec:failure}.
Reweighting the two rows from $1/2$ down to $1/128$ of the objective, FB's optimum keeps the full gap down to weight $1/8$, retains $19\%$ at $1/16$, and is at zero from $1/64$; the rows' share of the squared objective falls to zero over the same range, and the exact gap is constant by construction.
Adding ordinary states instead, with uniform row weights, FB's optimum loses the gap entirely once four states are added and never recovers it up to $64$.
TD-trained FB follows its optimum and selects the wrong action in every run from weight $1/32$.
The objective cost of a rank-$3$ fit that keeps the gap does not vanish (it converges to about a fifth of the optimum), so what disappears with rarity is the incentive to pay it, exactly as the lemma states.
The normalized model of \cref{sec:method} keeps the whole gap at every weight and every number of added states at the same rank, both at its optimum and, up to the noise of a rarer training signal, under TD training.
This is the separate claim of \cref{sec:method}: the rare distinction costs the linear family a rank direction and costs the normalized family nothing.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/rare_bottleneck/fig_rare_bottleneck.pdf}
    \caption{
    \textbf{A rare bottleneck under squared reconstruction.}
    Rank $3$ on the process of \cref{fig:didactic-mdp}.
    Left: fraction of the exact action gap at state $7$ preserved as the weight of the two state-$7$ rows in the objective decreases (lines: optimum; hollow markers: TD-trained, ten runs).
    Middle: the two rows' share of the squared objective, and the relative objective cost of a rank-$3$ FB fit that keeps the gap.
    Right: the same gap fraction as ordinary states are added with uniform weights.
    }
    \label{fig:rare-bottleneck}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Rank at uniform weights.}

At rank $3$, the exact successor measure assigns values $2.20$ and $0.80$ to the two actions at state $7$.
Trained by TD, FB predicts $1.04$ and $1.01$, making the actions nearly indistinguishable, while \name{} predicts $2.00$ and $0.62$.
Across ranks, TD-trained FB preserves only $1\%$-$5\%$ of the exact action gap at ranks $2$ and $3$ and requires rank $6$ out of nine states to recover $99\%$.
\name{} already preserves $53\%$ at rank $2$ and at least $99\%$ from rank $3$ onward.
The population optimum of FB's squared loss loses the gap at rank $2$, where Lemma~\ref{lem:shared-successor} applies, but retains it from rank $3$; the additional loss at ranks $3$ to $5$ arises when the same objective is optimized through its bootstrapped target rather than against the exact measure.
Normalizing the backward features to unit norm does not remove the failure.

Thus, FB can capture the dominant successor structure while losing the difference needed for the correct action, whereas \name{} preserves that difference with substantially lower rank, both at the optimum and under training.

\paragraph{Which of the three changes matters.}
The same process lets us separate the three changes of \cref{sec:method}.
\Cref{fig:toy-ablation} repeats the rank sweep for five critics that share the exact transitions, the continuation policy, and the rank: FB; FB fitting the normalized target $(1-\gamma)M$ with the same squared loss; the softmax parameterization fitted with squared error on probabilities; \name{}; and \name{} read out through FB's linear shortcut $F^\top z$ instead of by integration.
Normalizing the target alone changes nothing: the squared fit of $(1-\gamma)M$ is the squared fit of $M$ rescaled, and its action gap is identical at every rank, at the optimum and under training.
The gap is preserved once the bilinear score is placed inside a softmax, under either loss: at the population optimum both fits retain the whole gap at every rank, including rank $2$ where the linear fit reverses the action, and under TD training both reach it from rank $3$.
The loss placed on top of the softmax is therefore not what preserves the decision; cross-entropy is the choice under which the finite-sample update is the Bellman update of Proposition~\ref{prop:density-bellman}, not the source of the gain.
Reading the softmax model through the linear shortcut loses the gap at every rank, since $F^\top B$ is no longer the density: the integration readout is required by the parameterization, not a separate choice.
A soft immediate target in place of the one-hot term caps the preserved gap near $0.77$ at every rank, which is why the one-hot term is used.
Every arm retains at least $86\%$ of the squared mass of the successor matrix at rank $3$, so none of these differences is visible in aggregate reconstruction.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/toy_ablation/fig_toy_ablation.pdf}
    \caption{
    \textbf{Separating the three changes on the bottleneck process.}
    Fraction of the exact action gap at state $7$ preserved, against representation rank, for FB, FB with the normalized target, the softmax parameterization with squared error, \name{}, \name{} with the linear readout, and \name{} with a soft immediate target.
    Left: population optimum. Right: TD-trained, mean and 95\% interval over five seeds.
    }
    \label{fig:toy-ablation}
\end{figure}

