\subsection{Manipulation control across benchmarks}
\label{sec:exp-manipulation}

If the bottleneck failure identified above contributes to manipulation performance, then improving the successor estimate should improve control on tasks that require precise interactions with objects.
We test this first on OGBench, where FB, \name{}, HILP~\citep{park-iclr24a}, and RLDP~\citep{jajoo-iclr26a} use the same offline data and evaluation protocol. %%AZ.9.12: please include a baselines section that explains these baselines and why they were chosen.

The top block of \cref{tab:manipulation} shows that \name{} improves over FB from $45.6$ to $81.3$ on \texttt{cube-single}, from $2.2$ to $30.4$ on \texttt{cube-double}, and from $10.2$ to $52.7$ on \texttt{scene}.
It also exceeds HILP and RLDP on these three domains.
All successor-based methods remain near floor on \texttt{puzzle-3x3}, indicating that changing the successor objective alone does not solve every source of difficulty in manipulation.

We next ask whether these gains are specific to OGBench.
The lower blocks of \cref{tab:manipulation} evaluate the same FB and \name{} comparison on independently collected MetaWorld and ManiSkill datasets, which differ in their dynamics, state spaces, and control interfaces.
\name{} improves over FB on all eight tasks, with particularly large gains on bin picking, cube picking, and peg lifting.
The improvement therefore extends beyond the OGBench cube environments and appears across several manipulation systems.

\subsection{Transfer to rewards specified after pretraining}
\label{sec:reward-transfer}


The main benefit of a successor representation is that the same learned model can be reused when the downstream reward changes.
Our change to the successor-learning objective should therefore improve its approximation without sacrificing this property.

We test this by specifying rewards only after pretraining.
All representation and policy parameters remain frozen.
For each reward, we evaluate it on $10^4$ states from the offline dataset, infer the corresponding task index from these reward-labelled states, and execute the resulting policy without additional gradient updates or environment interaction.

The evaluation rewards are the OGBench task rewards, which measure completion of object conditions such as cube positions, button states, and drawer or window positions.
These rewards are not used during pretraining, which instead uses goal-reaching tasks sampled from future dataset states and random task indices.
Moreover, on \texttt{scene}, \texttt{cube-double}, and \texttt{puzzle-3x3}, the inferred evaluation-task indices are no closer to the pretraining goal indices than random task vectors.
This makes the reward-inference evaluation a test of whether the frozen representation can be reused for objectives that were not part of its pretraining tasks.

\cref{tab:reward-transfer} compares reward inference with direct goal conditioning on the same frozen checkpoints.
The latter removes reward-to-task inference from the evaluation and therefore helps separate limitations of task inference from limitations of the learned successor representation.
Reward inference reaches $81.3$, $30.4$, and $52.7$ on \texttt{cube-single}, \texttt{cube-double}, and \texttt{scene}, compared with $74.9$, $33.9$, and $59.7$ under direct goal conditioning.
The two routes therefore give comparable performance despite using different ways of specifying the downstream task.
The same frozen successor representation can thus be reused when the reward is introduced only after pretraining.
The OGBench task rewards are, however, still functions of a target configuration, so this comparison alone does not show that the model supports different preferences over future states.

\paragraph{Rewards that are not goal-reaching.}
To test reuse beyond target configurations, we evaluate the same frozen checkpoints on seven state rewards that no goal encodes (\cref{fig:reward-families}; definitions and constants in Appendix~\ref{app:reward-families}): a reward for the cube being anywhere in a region of the table; a reward for either of two distant regions; the same two regions with weights $1$ and $0.3$ and then swapped, on the same checkpoint; a reward for lifting the cube with no target position; the negative distance to a target, compared with the sparse success reward for the same target; and, on \texttt{cube-double}, a reward for moving one cube into a region while penalizing any displacement of the other, and a reward for either cube reaching a region.
Each reward is evaluated on $10^4$ dataset states, the task vector is inferred, and the frozen policy is scored by the mean per-step value of the requested reward over $20$ episodes, against a random-action floor and the behavior-cloning flow policy.
The region rewards separate the methods: \name{} places the cube in the rewarded region on about $60\%$ of steps against $10\%$ for FB, and the two density retrofits lift their hosts in the same way (HILP $0.48\to0.65$, RLDP $0.23\to0.42$).
The preference flip is complete for every method above the floor: swapping the weights moves the time spent from region A to region B, and the lower-weighted region is never visited.
On \texttt{cube-double}, \name{} is the only method that satisfies the conjunction, moving the first cube into its region on $78\%$ of steps while the second cube stays in place on $87\%$; FB and RLDP move the wrong cube and score below the do-nothing floor, and HILP leaves the second cube alone but never moves the first.
The lift reward does not separate methods, because a third of the dataset states already hold the cube in the air.
The dense-distance reward gives a worse policy than the sparse success reward for the same target on every method, which is expected of a task vector that is linear in the reward: a dense reward spreads the fit across states that do not distinguish the target.
The frozen model therefore supports rewards with different preferences over future states, not only re-encodings of a single target configuration.

