    \section{Isolating the Failure of FB}
    \label{sec:exp-failure}
\aditya{Move to appendix ?}

% The improvements in successor estimation translate into higher task performance, with larger gains on tasks requiring the interactions for which prediction accuracy improves.
The aggregate results in Table~\ref{tab:manipulation} show that \name{} improves manipulation performance, but do not explain why FB fails.
We therefore examine the frozen \texttt{cube-single} and \texttt{cube-double} checkpoints and ask two questions: 
\begin{inparaenum}
    \item where in the interaction does the policy fail? and
    \item does the critic correctly predict the consequences of its actions at those states?
\end{inparaenum}

We first divide each evaluation episode by the first stage at which it fails, following the interaction phases of \citet{mohan-rlbrew26a}: approach, acquisition, maintain, transport, or success.
The left panel of \Cref{fig:failure} shows that FB's failures on \texttt{cube-single} occur predominantly after the cube has been acquired, during maintain and transport.
\name{} fails somewhat more often during acquisition, but rarely fails once the cube is securely held.
On \texttt{cube-double}, both methods fail more often overall, but FB's failures are again concentrated after acquisition.
This places the main performance gap at the interactions highlighted by the bottleneck argument in Section~\ref{sec:failure}.

We next test whether the critic is inaccurate at these states.
From the same rollouts, we restore recorded simulator states from three stages of the interaction, approach, in-hand, and carrying, and compare each critic's predicted distribution over future cube positions with rollouts of its own policy.
We also evaluate a common set of candidate actions at each state and compare the critic's action ranking with their Monte-Carlo values.
Full details of the restored-state protocol and candidate actions are given in \aditya{Add Appendix}

The right panels of \Cref{fig:failure} show that FB's predicted cube distribution remains close to the dataset distribution across all three state types and differs substantially from the futures realized by its current policy.
Its ranking of candidate actions is also close to chance, including at carrying states where the choice of action determines whether the object remains secured.

